Given any $n\times n$ matrix $A$, any positive integer $k$, and any nonnegative integers $p(1),q(1),\dots,p(k),q(k)$, we may define $$M_n(\mathbf{p};\mathbf{q})\, 
=\, \frac{1}{n}\, \operatorname{tr}[A_n^{p(1)} (A_n^*)^{q(1)} \cdots A_n^{p(k)} (A_n^*)^{q(k)}]\, ,$$ for $\mathbf{p}=(p(1),\dots,p(k))$, $\mathbf{q}=(q(1),\dots,q(k))$. Notice that $M_0=1$. As an example, consider $$\begin{gathered}
M_n((2,2);(2,2))\, = \\ \, \frac{1}{n}\, \sum_{j_1,\dots,j_8=1}^n a_n(j_1,j_2) a_n(j_2,j_3) \overline{a}_n(j_4,j_3) \overline{a}_n(j_5,j_4) a_n(j_5,j_6) a_n(j_6,j_7)
\overline{a}_n(j_8,j_7) \overline{a}

[]{#sec:Expected label="sec:Expected"}

Since the moments $M_n(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{q})$ satisfy concentration of measure, one is primarily only interested in their expectations. The next quantity we introduce is also defined just for the expectation. (Studying its distribution may be interesting, but we will not comment on this, here.) It is the expected overlap function of Chalker and Mehlig, introduced in [@CM] and further studied by them in [@MC].

